A||N||9||Confederado120||V8 & V6 swap Ca. smog questions||||1||
Z||000000||Confederado120||08-20-2001||04:01 AM||||I live in SF Bay area. Plan on building a engine swap Fiero, 1988 model. Has any forum members out there built one that they were able to get past the BAR referee? If so what engine & trans combo are you running? I am considering a V8 Archie kit with TPI. But a 3800sc is a possibilty. If I go with a 3800 do I have to use a auto trans? Also if I go small block auto, is there a trans upgrade option? if anyone out there can shed some light on my options as far as what I can and cant do smog wise it wold be much appreciated.<P>Thanks, Walt||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||08-20-2001||11:27 AM||||I believe terryk is the resident expert on engine swaps and CA emmisions. He has a 3800SC. Seems the rule of thumb to pass is to use an engine/tranny/computer combination from one vehicle. Do a search on his userid and you should find out more.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Orville||08-20-2001||12:05 PM||||Yes, Terry's the man. Also, try<BR>championmotorcars.com/ca_smog.htm.<P>About a year ago, I got a swap of a<BR>3800 series II sc from a 96 bonneville<BR>(obdII) passed by the carb ref's. It <BR>was a complete transplant - engine,<BR>trans, all smog related equipment.<BR>Without going into all gory details,<BR>it was a hassle.<P>I'm planning to swap an lt1 from a 93<BR>z28 or firebird formula into my 87 gt.<BR>The carb problem here is the necessity<BR>of switching the lt1 to the getrag <BR>transaxle. The regulations are fuzzy<BR>in this regard and I couldn't get a<BR>definitive answer from the carb gurus<BR>I spoke with.<P>Lots of luck. Please keep us informed<BR>of your progress. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||08-20-2001||02:08 PM||||The rules are:<P>1. The engine must be from a CA car. To check that, they want the VIN of the donor car. From that they get the option list. YF5 (I think that's it) is CA emissions equipment. It must be there. Now the question is, will they check that the VIN you gave them matches the VIN stamped on the engine? It seems that most don't bother to check. I was told "Don't make me lie. You lie." It's best to get an engine from a California car to avoid any problems.  Whatever you do, have a VIN from a CA donor car. They also use the VIN for the scanner to check that it goes closed loop, no codes are stored, etc.<P>2. Install all of the emission equipment listed in the Mitchell's Automotive manual. This is where they get the list of emissions equipment to check. Go to any smog station and look up the engine you want to install. Get that list of equipment. Put it all in. Make it all work. Air pumps and cat injection tubes are required with TPI engines!<P>3. You can not install an engine/trans combination that was never offered in California. Therefore, a 3800 and manual trans is not allowed. A TPI with a manual is allowed but be careful. In some years the 5.7L was not available with a manual in CA. The 5.0L was. Again, know the year of the engine, look it up in the Mitchell's manual and write down the equipment list. Confirm that it shows a manual trans with that engine.<P>4. The Check Engine light must work.<P>5. The year of the engine must be of the same or newer year.<P>6. An interesting note is the trans. It seems that BAR does not care if you put a 5.7L TPI on a FWD trans. Just stick with the automatic/manual trans rules.<P>7. Contrary to mass-hysteria, BAR is actually very supportive of the swaps. Daily, these folks have to deal with jerks trying to get smoke puking crap and liquid solder connection swaps past them. When they see a nice/interesting swap come in, they really get into it. The emissions are usually lower (new engine) and it breaks up an otherwise boring day.<P>Find out where the closest referee is and go down and talk with them. Review the swap and get the details. Treat them like pro's and they are great to work with.<P>There are some other *grey* rules such as the exhaust. You're supposed to use the stock manifolds, but the routing down to the cat can vary (if the donor car had two cats, you're installing two cats....). After the cat is a don't-care. For a DOHC swap, we used a front manifold on the rear. This allowed us to fabricate a Fiero style crossover. On an SC swap, I cut off the down pipe on the rear manifold, sealed it and again fabricated a Fiero style crossover pipe.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000004||Orville||08-20-2001||06:57 PM||||Again Terry, you're most helpful and informative, thanks.<P>A couple of other cautions, however.<BR>On an lt1 swap, after 93 gm introduced<BR>a 2 & 3 gear lockout which under certain<BR>conditions forces a shift from 1st to 4th<BR>in manual transmissions. They did this<BR>in order to meet the cafe mileage require-<BR>ments. It is readily bypassed but if you<BR>get a ref who's aware of it he'll fail<BR>your vehicle.<P>Another point is that in the more recent<BR>year vehicles the pcm monitors and sets<BR>dtc's (data trouble codes) for many "events"<BR>not related to engine performance or<BR>emissions control. Even though the check<BR>engine light isn't lit for all these dtc's,<BR>when the ref detects them with his scan tool<BR>your vehicle will fail the test. That's what<BR>a carb representative told me. Please check<BR>to see if that's really the policy or if<BR>perhaps this representative was mistaken <BR>(possible?).<P>Also, Terry, I'm sorry not to share your<BR>benign opinion of the carb "folks" but<BR>maybe we're just a couple of blind men<BR>examining different parts of an elephant.<BR>In my case, the ref's couldn't scan the<BR>pcm in the swap because they hadn't updated <BR>the program in their snap on scan tool and<BR>were too ignorant/incompetent to realize<BR>they needed to. Instead they wanted me to<BR>bring to their station at my expense a<BR>mechanic who had already scanned the pcm <BR>in my vehicle with his snap on scan tool. I refused that and after I made some phone<BR>calls, they managed to get their scan tool<BR>working. At my 4th appointment with them, <BR>they reluctantly passed the vehicle after<BR>trying their damndest (I was watching) to<BR>unsuccessfully get the pcm to post a dtc so<BR>they could fail it. Maybe I just need to<BR>meet some of those nice ref's you know.   ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Confederado120||08-20-2001||08:19 PM||||Thanks for the info. I am leaning towards the 3800sc auto now. Can anybody with experience recommend a kit?<P>Thanks, Walt||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||08-20-2001||09:11 PM||||Oh, I'm sure there are dickheads out there. <P>Like I said, the Mitchell's manuals is the list they use here.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||08-21-2001||03:06 AM||||well, a smoking steaming pile of ****  older than 1977 gets to drive, but my Northstar fiero doesn't.  Because I have a manual tranny.  Nevermind that the engine is far cleaner than the 2.8.<P>Something just is WRONG with that, laws, refs, or no.<P>I often am told I am "not allowed to play emmisions engineer." bite me.  The car is cleaner under all conditions, and if I had $100grand or so to test as an auto manufactuer, I would prove it.<P>I don't think the kinds of things happening are in kind with the spirit of the laws/regs, but the fact remains that CA is a bad place for improving the emmisions of your vehicle.<P>I'm not sure what my workaround will be yet, it may be to swap in a 2.8 on cradle every 2 years.... (I might have it apart about that often for clutch replacement anyway :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000008||terryk||08-21-2001||09:59 AM||||Artherd,<P>I understand your sentiment, but that's just how it works. There's more to emissions than meets the eye.<P>So what are the emission levels of your NS? Is it pretty clean? My SC's seem to be pretty good.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||08-21-2001||09:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Artherd,<P>I understand your sentiment, but that's just how it works. There's more to emissions than meets the eye.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, I know. Most of it is temporal, ie how long it takes the Cats to light off, o2 sensor warmup time, etc.   In LA the smog tests already involve dyno time....... (and cost about $100)<P>Northstar hasn't been run to the smog guys yet. I'm trying to find the *dumbest* one I can, and put a "2.8l V6" badge on the northstar, and get two black plug wires and such :) :)<P>We'll see, it should be intresting at least to see what the little sniffer pipe says.<P>I'm also going to be using an aftermarket computer, so real uncharted territory emmisions wise here. (there will of course be two catalysts and all that good stuff. I don't like breathing raw gas any more than the guys behind me probally do ;0)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
